## Cohesion Audit: `src/blog/people/drafts/Mel-Robbins.md`

**Before: 7/10 → After: 9/10**

### Central Thesis
Mel Robbins is a Type 3 who escaped the performance trap by making the confession itself the performance — and the one place the confession stops is the authorship of her biggest idea.

### Thread Map (post-fix)

| # | Section | Contribution → next |
|---|---|---|
| — | Hook (turtleneck / voir dire) | Establishes the trade: say the humiliating thing first. Plants the withheld story. |
| 1 | Personality type | Names the type; the "I'm an attorney" quote is the load-bearing evidence → next section complicates it |
| 2 | Walked Away From the Law | Legal Aid proves the label answer isn't the whole answer; ready-fire-aim → the crash |
| 3 | Pot Roast Morning | 2008 collapse, 3→9 slide, 5 Second Rule as survival device → but nobody had told her why |
| 4 | Waited 47 Years | The diagnosis moves the defect from character to wiring; that relief is the product |
| — | Rabbit Hole | Wing/subtype/arrows/counter-typing, sealed off |
| 5 | The Mel Robbins Show | Thesis stress-test: she confesses the failure and reissues it as a credential → confession applied to others |
| 6 | Advice Sounds So Dumb | Empathy turn: simplicity is a spec, because complexity failed her → she concedes every charge |
| 7 | Let Them, Told Three Ways | The one charge she doesn't concede. Blind spot inside the legacy |
| 8 | The Sentence | What she gives away without a byline. Courtroom callback |

| Check | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Through-Line Clarity | 8/10 | 9/10 |
| Promise-Delivery Match | 7/10 | 9/10 |
| Reader Mental Model | 7/10 | 9/10 |
| Section Transitions | 6/10 | 9/10 |
| Internal Paragraph Flow | 9/10 | 9/10 |

### The one real break (fixed)

The second pass planted "Hold that pattern next to the one story where it stops" at the end of the **talk-show** section — but the next section is "Why Mel Robbins's Advice Sounds So Dumb." A reader takes that line as a promise about what comes next, and instead gets a defense of her advice. The breadcrumb was aimed two slots past its target.

Moved it to the end of the advice section, where the very next H2 pays it off. The talk-show section now bridges into the advice critique on its actual shared logic: *"Saying the damaging thing before anyone else can is the move she worked out in voir dire. Her critics have a bigger one for her to say."*

### Other changes (6)

- **Diagnosis → "Walked Away"**: the H2 re-asks a question the previous section already answered. Now framed as the complication: "The label answer is true and it is not the whole answer."
- **"Walked Away" → pot roast**: "nine years out of the courtroom" carries the 1999 exit into 2008 without claiming it caused the collapse.
- **Pot roast → ADHD**: the section opened cold on "Her son was being evaluated" right after a 2011 TEDx beat. Added the 2015 marker, which also pays off the "another sixteen years before anyone told her why" fuse from two sections earlier.
- **ADHD → talk show**: "four years after the diagnosis" keeps the reader oriented across the collapsible Rabbit Hole.
- **Let Them → closing**: "she could not let them keep it" now hands off to "There is one thing she gives away without needing her name on it."

### Preserved
Frontmatter, all four ledgers, all H2 wording, TL;DR, the Rabbit Hole, every internal link, the cold open, the "Told Three Ways" form, the empathy turn, and the closing callback. Bridging language only — no new arguments, no voice edits.

### Two things to note
- **Word count**: my additions (~65 words) pushed the body from ~3,986 to 4,051, tripping blog-lint's warn threshold (4,050) by one word. Still well under the 4,500 fail ceiling. `0 fail, 2 warn` — the other warn (no `faqs` in frontmatter) is pre-existing; the enrich stage hasn't run.
- **Remaining structural call, yours**: the hook's promise still runs six sections before it pays off. Chronology is why, and it's a defensible trade, but that fuse is now carried by one TL;DR bullet and one breadcrumb. If it still feels long, the fix is moving "Told Three Ways" ahead of "Sounds So Dumb" — which costs you the empathy turn landing before the prosecution.
